  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nEarly this morning a weak shortwave was rounding the apex of the \nupper level ridge over the region leading to thunderstorms across \nthe International Border. The warm and moist air mass that has been \nparked over the region the past few days gave way to plenty of fuel \nfor these storms to fire off. Ventilation was a bit more nebulous \nwith bulk shear values of around 30 kts. However, based on the radar \npresentation sub severe hail has likely been observed with \noccasional quarters at times. While this activity will wane \nthrough the morning hours, another shortwave moving across the \nRed River Valley will likely bring another dose of showers and \nstorms for northern MN later this morning. \n\nTrying to pick out the perfect solution for today will be nearly \nimpossible. The big picture is that we still have a very warm and \nmoist air mass over the region. Isentropic lift paired with any \nsubtle disturbances aloft could generate additional showers and \nstorms throughout the day. The best forcing will initially be out \nwest across the Northern Plains as a stout mid level shortwave \npresses east. This feature will also help to propel a low pressure \nsystem across the Midwest. The better rainfall still looks to be to \nour south closer to the low pressure system moving through southern \nMN. That's not to say we won't see any activity through the \novernight hours. There is another system moving through Ontario \nwhich will have a cold front sliding through our neck of the woods \nto help generate some activity as well. But the overall evolution \nlargely depends on how much the region gets worked over during the \ndaylight hours. \n\nThrough the day and even the overnight hours the thermodynamics \nshould remain favorable with instability in excess of 2000 J/kg \nexpected. The limiting factors remain our lifting mechanism, which \nincreases this afternoon and evening. And the kinematics as our bulk \nshear values needed for proper storm maintenance remain lacking. As \nsuch the SPC still has the region highlighted in a marginal risk \n(level 1 of 5) through tomorrow morning. Primary hazards would be \nlarge hail and damaging winds. Excessive rainfall is still outlined \nas a marginal threat from WPC as well, but given that the better \nforcing is to our south this seems less impactful. PWATs will still \nbe over 1.50\" which is above the 90th percentile, so storms that do \ngenerate could disperse some healthy amounts of rain. \n\nFriday: \n\nRain and storm activity will linger through the morning hours with \nsome guidance suggesting a final push of storms possible in the \nafternoon across NW WI as the cold front exits the region. \nTemperatures will cool into the lower 80s and dewpoints will also \ndrop out of the 70s and back into the 60s providing a nice reprieve \nfrom the muggy weather.  \n\nWeekend-Next Week:\n\nQuiet weather is expected through the weekend as a surface high \npressure slides across Ontario and through the Great Lakes. \nTemperatures will be slightly higher than seasonable averages with \nhighs forecasted to be in the 80s. Fortunately dewpoints will stay in \nthe 60s keeping the oppressive heat at bay. Monday starts to warm up \nwith high climbing into the upper 80s as southerly return flow from \nthe departing high pressure streams back into the Northland. This \nwarm up will be breif as another system slides in late Monday \nthrough Tuesday bringing more rain and storm chances and cooler \nweather on the backside.",
